window.ESSAY_COPY.en = {
  'doc.title': 'Ten Ideas Inside One Phone · How It Works',
  'doc.desc': 'A phone knows where it is, which way is up, and where your finger is, and it can pull a movie out of thin air. Ten features, and behind each one is a tiny measurement plus a way to pick the signal out of the noise. Each chapter comes with a model you can play with.',
  'back': '← Knowledge index',
  'lang.group': 'Language',
  'lang.zh': '中',
  'lang.en': 'EN',

  'hero.kicker': 'How it works · an essay you can play with',
  'hero.title': 'Ten Ideas Inside One Phone',
  'hero.sub': 'It knows where you are, which way is up, and where your finger is,<br>and it can pull a movie out of thin air.',
  'hero.lead': 'None of this is magic. In ten chapters I want to answer one question: for each thing a phone does, what is the one physical idea behind it?',
  'hero.lead2': 'You will find that the answer almost always has the same shape: <strong>the phone measures a tiny quantity, then uses a method to separate the signal from the noise.</strong> In each chapter I point out what those two things are, and give you a model you can drag around.',
  'tiny.gps.v': '1 nanosecond = 30 cm',
  'tiny.gps': 'The distance light travels in one nanosecond. GPS has to tell times this short apart.',
  'tiny.acc.v': 'About 10 nm',
  'tiny.acc': 'Roughly how far the silicon inside the accelerometer sinks under gravity when the phone lies flat.',
  'tiny.cam.v': '0.8 µm',
  'tiny.cam': 'The side length of one pixel in a phone camera. In the dark, only a few photons land on it per frame.',
  'tiny.radio.v': '−174 dBm/Hz',
  'tiny.radio': 'The thermal noise power per hertz of bandwidth at room temperature. A phone signal has to be stronger than this to be useful.',
  'hero.note': 'This page was written by an AI (Claude). Each model is a simplification made to see one mechanism clearly, not a measurement of any particular phone. Each chapter ends with a note on what it leaves out. Real-world figures in the text are sourced in BRIEF.md in this directory; where I could not find a source, I have labeled the number "order of magnitude."',

  'toc.aria': 'Contents',
  'toc.title': 'Contents',

  'part1.kicker': 'Part one',
  'part1.title': 'How a phone senses the world',
  'part1.p': 'The first four chapters cover four sensors. What they measure gets smaller at each step: nanoseconds, nanometers, capacitance below one picofarad, a handful of photons.',

  /* ───── 01 GPS ───── */
  'gps.title': 'Where you are: time is distance',
  'gps.law': 'A phone does not "see" where the satellites are. It measures how long their signals took to arrive. Measuring time requires a clock, and the phone\'s own clock is not accurate, so it treats the clock error as one more unknown and uses an extra satellite to solve for it.',
  'gps.p1': 'GPS satellites orbit at about 20,200 km, and each carries an atomic clock. They keep broadcasting "who I am, where I am, and what time it is." When a satellite is directly overhead, its signal takes about 67 ms to reach you. The phone subtracts the time the signal was sent from the time it arrived, multiplies by the speed of light, and gets the distance to that satellite.',
  'gps.p2': 'The difficulty is that light is very fast. In one nanosecond it travels 30 cm; in one microsecond, 300 m. The phone has only a cheap quartz crystal as its clock, and that clock is often off by a microsecond. If it were used directly, every distance would be wrong by hundreds of meters.',
  'gps.p3': 'The fix is not to assume the phone\'s clock is right, but to treat "how far off the clock is" as one more unknown to solve for. In the flat cross-section below, the unknowns are the east–west position, the height, and the clock error: three in all, so three satellites are needed. The real world has one more coordinate, so four are needed.',
  'gps.fig': 'Three satellites, three circles, and the phone\'s own clock',
  'gps.how': 'The picture is a close-up of the few hundred meters around you. The three lines are small pieces of circles centered on the three satellites (the top-left corner shows the Earth and the satellites to scale). Start with the default: the phone\'s clock is 300 ns fast, but the phone assumes its clock is correct, so the three circles do not meet at one point. Drag "clock error the phone assumes" and watch the red triangle shrink; then press "let the phone solve it." Next add a little timing noise and push the satellites into one corner of the sky.',
  'gps.cvAria': 'Close-up of the area around you: distance circles of three satellites and where they intersect',
  'gps.lg.s1': 'Circle for satellite 1',
  'gps.lg.s2': 'Circle for satellite 2',
  'gps.lg.s3': 'Circle for satellite 3',
  'gps.lg.you': 'Your true position',
  'gps.lg.miss': 'Area where the three circles fail to meet',
  'gps.c.bias': 'Actual amount the phone\'s clock is off',
  'gps.c.guess': 'Clock error the phone assumes',
  'gps.c.solve': 'Let the phone solve it',
  'gps.c.noise': 'Timing noise per satellite',
  'gps.c.spread': 'Angle the satellites spread across the sky',
  'gps.o.ns': '{v} ns',
  'gps.o.deg': '±{v}°',
  'gps.r.miss': 'Greatest distance between the three intersections (m)',
  'gps.r.clock': 'Clock error the phone solved for',
  'gps.r.err': 'How far the solved position is from you (m)',
  'gps.r.scale': 'Width of the picture (m)',
  'gps.r.none': '—',
  'gps.cv.scale': '{m} m',
  'gps.say.miss': 'The three circles do not meet at one point. The greatest distance between their intersections is <em>{miss} m</em>. The clock error the phone assumes differs from the actual one by {off} ns; in that time light travels {dist} m, so all three distances are too long or too short by about that much.',
  'gps.say.missNoise': 'Your guessed clock error now matches the real one, yet the three circles are still <em>{miss} m</em> apart: each satellite\'s timing has a little noise, so the three cannot all agree. Press "let the phone solve it" to see how it finds a compromise answer.',
  'gps.say.meet': 'The three circles meet at one point, which is your position. The clock error the phone solved for is <em>{clk} ns</em>, the same as the one you set. It was never told how far its clock was off; the data from the three satellites worked it out.',
  'gps.say.noise': 'The circles meet again, but the meeting point is not you: it is <em>{err} m</em> away. Each satellite\'s timing is wrong by only {noise} ns ({nm} m), but with the satellites spread ±{spread}°, that gets magnified into a position error this large. The more tightly bunched the satellites, the more the error is magnified.',
  'gps.after1': 'In the default state, a 300 ns clock error makes the three circles miss each other by up to about 136 m. Press "let the phone solve it" and it solves for exactly 300 ns; the three circles merge into one point, and the position error is zero. That is the whole trick: <strong>the clock error is the same for every satellite</strong>, so it is not random noise but a number that can be solved for.',
  'gps.after2': 'Now add 10 ns of timing noise (3 m). With the satellites spread to ±70°, the position error is about 5.6 m; squeezed to ±15°, it becomes about 28 m. The same timing error gets magnified more when the geometry is worse. This is called the <strong>dilution of precision</strong> (DOP). Between tall buildings you can see only a narrow strip of sky, which is one reason phone positioning jumps around in cities.',
  'gps.rel': 'There is one more, smaller correction. According to Einstein\'s theory, a satellite is farther from the Earth and feels weaker gravity, so its clock runs fast by about 45.7 µs per day; but it also moves fast, so its clock runs slow by about 7.2 µs per day. Subtracting the two, the satellite clock runs about 38.5 µs per day faster than clocks on the ground. (This is what the model calculates from the satellite\'s orbital radius; the measured value given by Ashby 2003 corresponds to about 38.6 µs per day.) Light travels 11.5 km in 38.5 µs, so the satellite clocks are deliberately slowed a little before launch. For why a moving clock runs slow, see <a href="../../../physics/modern/relativity/#step0">the light-clock step on the Special Relativity page</a>; for why a satellite at this altitude goes round the Earth once every 12 hours (11 h 58 min), see <a href="../../../earth/astronomy/gravity-orbits/#step4">Gravity and Orbits</a>.',
  'gps.limit': 'This model leaves out: the third coordinate; errors of a few meters (order of magnitude) caused by the atmosphere slowing the signal; "multipath" signals reflected off tall buildings; and errors in the satellites\' own clocks and orbits. Real receivers use four or more satellites and least-squares fitting, rather than just the bare minimum of equations.',

  /* ───── 02 accelerometer ───── */
  'acc.title': 'Which way is up: a tiny piece of silicon on a spring',
  'acc.law': 'An accelerometer does not measure motion directly. It measures how much force the spring must exert to hold up a small piece of silicon. So when the phone is at rest, it reads gravity; when it is in free fall, it reads zero.',
  'acc.p1': 'Turn your phone sideways and the screen turns with it. The chip responsible is about the size of a fingernail. Inside it is a small piece of silicon hanging from fine silicon springs, with comb-like electrodes around it. When the silicon moves, the capacitance between it and the electrodes changes, and the chip reads that capacitance.',
  'acc.p2': 'When the phone lies still on a table, the spring has to push the silicon upward, or the silicon would fall. The chip reads that upward push: pointing up, with a magnitude of 9.81 m/s². Tilt the phone and this push is split between the phone\'s own two axes; the ratio of the two readings tells the phone how many degrees it is tilted.',
  'acc.fig': 'Tilt the phone, or let it drop',
  'acc.how': 'Drag the tilt and watch how the gold reading is divided between the phone\'s two axes. The silicon mass (blue) is drawn with its displacement magnified about ten million times. Then press "release" and see what the reading becomes during the half second of falling.',
  'acc.cvAria': 'Proof mass, springs and reading directions inside the phone',
  'acc.lg.g': 'Direction of gravity',
  'acc.lg.read': 'Accelerometer reading (spring force)',
  'acc.lg.mass': 'Silicon mass, displacement magnified about ten million times',
  'acc.c.tilt': 'Phone tilt',
  'acc.c.drop': 'Release',
  'acc.c.dropOn': 'Catch',
  'acc.o.deg': '{v}°',
  'acc.r.x': 'Along the phone\'s horizontal axis (m/s²)',
  'acc.r.y': 'Along the phone\'s vertical axis (m/s²)',
  'acc.r.mag': 'Magnitude of reading (m/s²)',
  'acc.r.disp': 'Silicon displacement (nm, order of magnitude)',
  'acc.say.still': 'The phone is still, tilted {tilt}°. The total magnitude of the reading is always <em>9.81</em>, always pointing straight up; it is just split into {x} and {y} along the phone\'s two axes. The phone works out which way is up from the ratio of these two numbers.',
  'acc.say.fall': 'In free fall, the reading is <em>0</em>. The silicon falls together with the phone, so the spring does not need to hold it up. The accelerometer cannot sense gravity itself; it can only sense "something is holding me up."',
  'acc.mid1': 'The reading is zero in free fall. That is not a defect in the chip; it is simply what the chip measures: any force other than gravity that pushes on the phone. A bathroom scale in an elevator changing its reading is the same thing; see <a href="../../../physics/mechanics/newton-laws/#step5">the elevator step on the Newton\'s Laws of Motion page</a>. How far the silicon is pushed down depends on how stiff the spring is: the displacement equals the reading divided by (2π·resonant frequency)². The resonant frequency is taken to be on the order of several kilohertz (I use 5 kHz), and 1 g pushes the silicon down by only about 10 nm, smaller than a virus.',
  'acc.mid2': 'If it can measure acceleration, could you integrate the acceleration twice to work out how far the phone has moved? The model below does exactly that for a phone sitting motionless on a table.',
  'drift.fig': 'How far a motionless phone "travels" by integration',
  'drift.how': 'The phone is still, so the true acceleration is zero. But every accelerometer has a small zero-point offset and random noise. Drag the offset and noise, press "restart," and watch how the integrated position drifts further and further away. You can also tick "GPS correction once per second." The simulation runs at 4× speed.',
  'drift.cvAria': 'Position error from integration as a function of time',
  'drift.lg.est': 'Position from integration',
  'drift.lg.theory': 'Theoretical value from the offset alone, ½·b·t²',
  'drift.lg.gps': 'With GPS correction',
  'drift.c.bias': 'Zero-point offset b',
  'drift.c.noise': 'Random noise',
  'drift.c.gps': 'GPS correction once per second',
  'drift.c.reset': 'Restart',
  'drift.o.acc': '{v} m/s²',
  'drift.r.t': 'Elapsed time (s)',
  'drift.r.x': 'Position from integration (m)',
  'drift.r.th': '½·b·t² (m)',
  'drift.cv.t': 'Time (s) →',
  'drift.cv.m': '{v} m',
  'drift.say': 'The phone has not moved at all, yet the integration says it has traveled <em>{x} m</em>. The error grows with the square of time: double the time, and the error becomes four times as large.',
  'drift.say.gps': 'Pulling the integration back with a GPS position once per second keeps the error at roughly <em>{x} m</em>, instead of growing without limit.',
  'acc.after1': 'With an offset of 0.02 m/s² (about two thousandths of g; I picked this as an order of magnitude and did not look up any particular chip\'s specification), counting only the offset, the position is off by 1 m after 10 s and by 36 m after 60 s. The error is proportional to the square of time, which is why phones do not navigate with the accelerometer alone. It is good for short periods and for direction; for long-term position, GPS takes over. Combined, correcting once per second keeps the error at a few meters. Real phones use smarter fusion methods, called Kalman filters; see <a href="../../../signal/control/kalman/#step2">prediction and update in the Kalman filter</a>. For why integration piles up small errors, see <a href="../../../math/calculus/integral/#step2">the v–t graph on the Definite Integral page</a>.',
  'acc.limit': 'Here there is only one axis, the offset is constant, the noise is white noise, and the phone does not rotate. A real phone must also subtract its rotation using a gyroscope, and its offset drifts with temperature.',

  /* ───── 03 touch ───── */
  'touch.title': 'Where your finger is: capacitance and interpolation',
  'touch.law': 'The finger does not need to press down. It just changes the capacitance of a mesh of electrodes under the glass. The position is not read from any single electrode; it is interpolated from the readings of several neighboring electrodes.',
  'touch.p1': 'Under the screen glass are two layers of transparent electrodes, one running horizontally and one vertically, forming something like a net. Neighboring electrodes are about 5 mm apart (order of magnitude). The control chip keeps sending signals along the horizontal lines and listens for them on the vertical lines. Your finger is a conductor; when it approaches, it draws away part of the electric field, so the capacitance at those crossing points drops slightly, by less than one picofarad (order of magnitude).',
  'touch.p2': 'But the electrodes are 5 mm apart, and yet you can place the cursor between two letters. The phone does not just look at which electrode reacts most strongly. Your finger covers several electrodes, and each changes a little; taking a weighted average by the size of each change gives a position much finer than the electrode spacing.',
  'touch.fig': 'Eight electrodes, one finger',
  'touch.how': 'Drag the finger position (you can also drag directly on the picture) and watch the readings of the eight electrodes (blue bars). The red triangle takes only the electrode with the strongest response; the gold triangle takes a weighted average of the peak and its two neighbors, three electrodes in all. The lower half shows the error of the two methods across the whole screen; the vertical line is your current position. Make the contact width smaller and watch the gold line begin to wobble.',
  'touch.cvAria': 'Electrode readings, finger position, and error curves for the two positioning methods',
  'touch.lg.sig': 'Reading of each electrode',
  'touch.lg.true': 'True finger position',
  'touch.lg.peak': 'Strongest electrode only',
  'touch.lg.cent': 'Weighted average of three electrodes',
  'touch.c.x': 'Finger position',
  'touch.c.w': 'Contact width',
  'touch.c.noise': 'Reading noise',
  'touch.o.mm': '{v} mm',
  'touch.o.noise': '{v}',
  'touch.r.peak': 'Strongest electrode only: error (mm)',
  'touch.r.cent': 'Weighted average: error (mm)',
  'touch.r.worst': 'Largest weighted-average error mid-panel at this width (no noise, mm)',
  'touch.cv.x': 'Position (mm) →',
  'touch.cv.pm': '{v} mm',
  'touch.say': 'The electrodes are 5 mm apart. Using only the strongest one, the error is <em>{pe} mm</em>; using a weighted average of three, the error is <em>{ce} mm</em>.',
  'touch.say.noisy': 'The electrodes are 5 mm apart. Without noise, at this position the strongest electrode alone is off by <em>{pe} mm</em> and the three-electrode average by <em>{ce} mm</em>. With noise the readings jitter from moment to moment; the two tiles above show the error at this instant.',
  'touch.say.lost': 'The finger sits exactly between two electrodes, and the contact is this narrow: each electrode\'s change is smaller than the noise. The controller then cannot tell whether anyone is touching at all, and the position it computes means nothing.',
  'touch.say.narrow': 'The contact is too narrow: the finger covers only one electrode, and the two neighbors barely respond, so there is nothing for the weighted average to average. The error curve starts to oscillate periodically, peaking at <em>{worst} mm</em>.',
  'touch.after1': 'With a contact width of 2.5 mm and no noise, using only the strongest electrode gives an error of at most 2.5 mm (half the spacing); the three-electrode weighted average gives at most about 0.13 mm in the middle of the panel, about one fortieth of the spacing. Right at the edges the error is much larger (the gold line curls up at both ends), because there is no electrode beyond the last one to weigh against. With the default noise added, the average error is about 0.16 mm. Set the width to 0.8 mm (a fine stylus tip), and the weighted-average error oscillates periodically up to about 1.9 mm: interpolation needs the signal to fall on several electrodes at once. This is also why the tips of capacitive styluses are usually broad and soft: with a fine tip, the signal lands on only one electrode. (An ordinary plastic pen is a different matter: it does not conduct, so it draws away no field at all.) The method is called <strong>centroid interpolation</strong>; it turns the spreading of the signal into an advantage.',
  'touch.limit': 'A real screen is a two-dimensional grid, with the mutual capacitance measured at each crossing point; the shape of the finger signal is also not the Gaussian curve I use. The controller also keeps tracking the "no touch" baseline all the time, subtracting interference from water droplets and chargers.',

  /* ───── 04 camera ───── */
  'cam.title': 'Light arrives one photon at a time: the camera',
  'cam.law': 'A photo taken in the dark looks blotchy not because the camera is broken but because there are too few photons. When you count N photons, the fluctuation is about √N; to halve the relative noise, you have to collect four times as much light.',
  'cam.p1': 'Photos taken at night always have a layer of colored grain, and night mode asks you to hold the phone steady for several seconds. The cause is not the lens but the light itself: light arrives as individual photons, and when each one arrives is random.',
  'cam.p2': 'Each pixel counts the photons that land on it during the exposure. Even if the scene does not change at all, one frame may count 9 photons and the next 13. When the average count is N, the typical size of the fluctuation is √N, so the relative noise is 1/√N. Phone pixels are very small: one 48-megapixel phone camera sensor announced in 2018 has pixels only 0.8 µm on a side.',
  'cam.fig': 'A dark street, a gray card',
  'cam.how': 'On the left is what the sensor sees. Each frame draws photons at random again, and the frames are then averaged. The gold box in the lower left holds a uniform gray card, where the noise is measured. On the right is how the noise on the gray card changes with the number of frames stacked: gold dots are measured values, the gray dashed line is 1/√N. Drag the light level, the pixel size and the number of frames to stack.',
  'cam.cvAria': 'Dim-light image after stacking multiple frames, and how noise changes with the number of frames',
  'cam.lg.meas': 'Noise measured on the gray card',
  'cam.lg.theory': 'Theoretical value 1/√N',
  'cam.c.light': 'Light level (photons per µm²)',
  'cam.c.pix': 'Pixel side length',
  'cam.c.frames': 'Frames stacked',
  'cam.c.reset': 'Shoot again',
  'cam.o.light': '{v}',
  'cam.o.um': '{v} µm',
  'cam.o.frames': '{v} frames',
  'cam.r.ph': 'Photons per pixel per frame on the gray card',
  'cam.r.n': 'Frames stacked so far',
  'cam.r.meas': 'Noise measured on the gray card',
  'cam.r.th': 'Theoretical value 1/√(total photons)',
  'cam.cv.frames': 'Frames →',
  'cam.cv.noise': 'Noise',
  'cam.say': 'On the gray card, each pixel receives on average only <em>{ph}</em> photons per frame. After stacking {n} frames, the total is about {tot}; the noise is <em>{meas}</em>, against a theoretical value of {th}.',
  'cam.after1': 'By default, at 20 photons per µm², a 0.8 µm pixel receives only about 13 photons per frame; on the gray card, only about 6.4, and the single-frame noise is about 39.5%. Stacking 16 frames brings the noise down to about 9.9%: multiply the frame count by 16 and the noise is divided only by 4. Doubling the pixel side to 1.6 µm quadruples the area; the single-frame noise is about 19.8%, the same as stacking 4 frames with 0.8 µm pixels. The sensor mentioned earlier does exactly this in the dark, combining each group of four adjacent pixels into one, which is equivalent to a 1.6 µm pixel.',
  'cam.after2': 'This noise is called <strong>shot noise</strong>. It comes from the act of counting itself, and no amount of good circuitry can remove it; you can only collect more photons: bigger pixels, longer exposures, or more frames. Night mode is the third option: it shoots many frames in quick succession and averages them after aligning. For why the fluctuation in a count is √N, see <a href="../../../probability/stochastic/poisson-process/#step2">the number of arrivals in a window in the Poisson process</a>; for why averaging N measurements shrinks the error only by √N, see <a href="../../../probability/statistics/central-limit-theorem/#step3">the precision cost in the central limit theorem</a>. Keep this model in mind; chapter six uses it.',
  'cam.limit': 'The model contains only photon noise. A real sensor also has readout-circuit noise and dark current; each pixel is covered by a red, green or blue filter and receives only part of the light; and hand shake and moving objects mean the frames must be aligned before they can be stacked.',

  'part2.kicker': 'Part two',
  'part2.title': 'How a phone shows you things',
  'part2.p': 'The next two chapters cover how a phone hands an image to your eyes, and how it packs that image into a small file. Both rely on knowing "which information is not needed at all."',

  /* ───── 05 screen ───── */
  'scr.title': 'Yellow pixels contain no yellow light',
  'scr.law': 'Your eye measures color with only three numbers. A screen does not need to make yellow light; it only needs to make those three numbers match what yellow light would produce.',
  'scr.p1': 'Look at a phone screen through a magnifier and you will see only tiny red, green and blue dots. Yellow on the screen is red and green dots lit at the same time. The retina has three kinds of cone cells that handle color, most sensitive at wavelengths of about 420, 534 and 563 nm (short, medium and long, labeled S, M and L). Light comes in, and each type of cone reports just one number: how strongly it was stimulated.',
  'scr.p2': 'This means the eye compresses an entire spectrum into three numbers. Two lights with completely different spectra look identical to you as long as those three numbers match. This is called <strong>metamerism</strong>. The whole capability of a screen rests on it.',
  'scr.fig': 'Matching a single-wavelength light with three subpixels',
  'scr.how': 'Top half: the white vertical line is the single-wavelength light to imitate; the gold curve is the spectrum actually emitted by mixing the three subpixels; the dashed lines are the sensitivities of the three cone types. Bottom half: how the three cone types respond to the two lights. The default is already set to yellow light at 580 nm. Drag the wavelength to around 500 nm, adjust red, green and blue yourself to chase it, then press "compute the best mix."',
  'scr.cvAria': 'Spectrum of single-wavelength light and subpixel mixture, and the responses of the three cone types',
  'scr.lg.target': 'Single-wavelength light to imitate',
  'scr.lg.mix': 'Light emitted by the subpixel mix',
  'scr.lg.cones': 'Sensitivity of the three cone types',
  'scr.c.lam': 'Wavelength of the single-wavelength light',
  'scr.c.r': 'Red subpixel (625 nm)',
  'scr.c.g': 'Green subpixel (530 nm)',
  'scr.c.b': 'Blue subpixel (460 nm)',
  'scr.c.solve': 'Compute the best mix',
  'scr.swatch': 'Roughly what this mix looks like on your screen',
  'scr.o.nm': '{v} nm',
  'scr.o.w': '{v}',
  'scr.r.mis': 'Largest difference among the three cone readings',
  'scr.r.need': 'Red · green · blue needed for an exact match',
  'scr.r.at': 'Intensity of the mixed light at this wavelength (percent of the subpixel peak)',
  'scr.cv.nm': 'Wavelength (nm)',
  'scr.say.match': 'The three cone types respond almost identically to the two lights, so you cannot tell them apart. But the mixed light at {lam} nm is only <em>{at}</em> of the subpixel peak: this color contains almost no light at that wavelength.',
  'scr.say.out': 'To match {lam} nm exactly, the red needed is <em>{neg}</em>, a negative number. A screen cannot emit negative light, so this color lies outside the screen\'s <strong>gamut</strong>, and can only be approximated.',
  'scr.say.adjust': 'The three cone readings still differ by <em>{mis}</em>. Keep adjusting red, green and blue, or press "compute the best mix."',
  'scr.after1': 'At 580 nm, the best mix is red 0.90, green 0.16, and blue almost zero (the exact solution needs blue −0.002, a tiny difference). The three cone types differ in response by less than 1%, but the mixed light at 580 nm is only about 0.4% of the subpixel peak: <strong>a yellow pixel contains almost no yellow light</strong>. Move to a cyan-green at 500 nm, and the exact match needs red −0.89. Strictly speaking, every pure spectral color lies outside the range the three primaries can mix; yellow is just exceptionally close, while cyan-green is far away.',
  'scr.after2': 'This also explains why "mixing light" and "mixing pigments" work in opposite ways. Light adds: red light plus green light stimulates all three cone types more, and the result looks like a brighter yellow. Pigments subtract: each pigment absorbs part of the light, so red and green pigments mixed together absorb almost everything, leaving a dark brownish mass. That is why printers use cyan, magenta and yellow inks.',
  'scr.limit': 'The cone curves use the pigment absorption template of Govardovskii et al. (2000), with peak values from Bowmaker and Dartnall (1980); they do not account for light filtered by the lens and macula. The subpixel spectra are schematic Gaussian shapes. The color swatch beside the chart converts the model\'s weights to a white-balanced screen and draws them with the primaries of your own screen, so it is only a rough impression.',

  /* ───── 06 compression ───── */
  'cmp.title': 'Why a photo of the sky is small',
  'cmp.law': 'How small a photo can be compressed depends on how easy it is to predict. The predictable parts take up almost no space; the unpredictable parts, including noise, cannot be made smaller by even one bit.',
  'cmp.p1': 'Two photos taken with the same phone, one of a clear sky and one of grass, can differ in file size by several times. The same number of pixels: where is the difference?',
  'cmp.p2': 'The basic method of compression is to first guess what the next pixel will be, and store only "how far off the guess was." The simplest guess is "the same as the pixel to the left." The sky is a smooth gradient, so the guess is almost always off by 0 or ±1, which can be recorded with very few bits. Each blade of grass is different, so no guess is accurate. On average each pixel needs at least a few bits, determined by the <strong>entropy</strong> of these "guess errors," which measures the average amount of surprise.',
  'cmp.fig': 'Guess first, then store only the surprises',
  'cmp.how': 'On the left is a 128×72 grayscale image; on the right is the distribution of how much each pixel differs from the one on its left. Choose sky or grass, then drag camera noise up and watch the distribution widen and the bits per pixel rise.',
  'cmp.cvAria': 'Image and histogram of its prediction errors',
  'cmp.c.scene': 'Choose a scene',
  'cmp.c.sky': 'Clear sky',
  'cmp.c.grass': 'Grass',
  'cmp.c.noise': 'Camera noise (gray levels)',
  'cmp.o.noise': '{v}',
  'cmp.r.bits': 'Bits needed per pixel (8 if uncompressed)',
  'cmp.r.mb': 'Converted to 12 megapixels, one channel (MB)',
  'cmp.r.ratio': 'How many times smaller than uncompressed',
  'cmp.cv.res': 'Prediction error',
  'cmp.say.sky': 'A smooth sky is almost always predicted correctly, needing only <em>{bits} bits</em> per pixel, which is {ratio} times smaller than the 8 bits of the uncompressed version.',
  'cmp.say.noisy': 'With noise added, each pixel needs <em>{bits} bits</em>. In theory, pure noise alone requires log₂(σ·√2·√(2πe)) ≈ {th} bits: those bits record random photons, and the compressor cannot tell them apart from detail.',
  'cmp.say.grass': 'Almost every pixel of grass is a surprise, needing <em>{bits} bits</em> per pixel. The "same as the pixel to the left" guess does not help here.',
  'cmp.after1': 'A clean sky needs about 0.47 bits per pixel; converted to one channel of a 12-megapixel image, that is about 0.7 MB, compared with 12 MB uncompressed. Raise the noise standard deviation to 4 gray levels and it needs about 4.5 bits, or 6.8 MB; grass needs about 7.2 bits. Returning to the camera model of chapter four: a photo taken in the dark is noisier, so <strong>night photos tend to have larger files</strong>. Noise is genuine information: it records which photons happened to arrive. It is just information you do not want. For how entropy is defined, see <a href="../../../probability/stochastic/entropy/#step2">the entropy workshop on the entropy page</a>; for how to build the shortest code from entropy, see <a href="../../../probability/stochastic/entropy/#step4">Huffman coding</a>.',
  'cmp.limit': 'This is a "lossless prediction" model, close to the idea behind PNG. The JPEG or HEIC files a phone actually stores are lossy: they apply a frequency transform and then discard details the eye does not care about, so real files are smaller and the numbers differ. But the point that "the harder to predict, the bigger, and noise cannot be squeezed out" still holds.',

  'part3.kicker': 'Part three',
  'part3.title': 'How a phone talks to the distant world',
  'part3.p': 'Three chapters cover radio. By the time a signal reaches you, it is so weak it is almost buried in noise; the phone must decide how fast to transmit, how to correct errors, and which base station to talk to.',

  /* ───── 07 radio ───── */
  'rad.title': 'Distance, noise and Shannon',
  'rad.law': 'Signal power falls off quickly with distance; and how many bits per second you can send depends only on the bandwidth and on how much stronger the signal is than the noise.',
  'rad.p1': 'The farther you are from a base station, the fewer signal bars you have, and video starts to stutter. In open terrain, the base station\'s power is spread over an ever larger sphere: double the distance and the power you receive is a quarter of what it was, 6 dB less. In a city, buildings and trees block the signal and the attenuation is faster: a common empirical model replaces "distance squared" with "distance to the power n." In open areas n = 2; the more cluttered the environment, the larger n, often between 2 and 4, and inside buildings it can be larger still.',
  'rad.p2': 'On the other side is noise. In any resistor at a temperature above absolute zero, electrons jitter around; at room temperature, every hertz of bandwidth carries about −174 dBm of thermal noise. In 1948 Shannon proved that a channel with bandwidth B and signal-to-noise ratio S/N can carry at most B·log₂(1 + S/N) bits per second without error. This is an upper limit, not an achievement of any particular technology.',
  'rad.fig': 'How fast you can transmit at each distance from the base station',
  'rad.how': 'The horizontal axis is distance to the base station and the vertical axis is the Shannon limit (both on logarithmic scales). The gold line is the bandwidth you choose; the blue line is a 5 MHz reference. Drag the distance and the attenuation exponent; then widen the bandwidth once near and once far, and compare how much the rate goes up.',
  'rad.cvAria': 'Shannon capacity as a function of distance',
  'rad.lg.cur': 'Bandwidth you chose',
  'rad.lg.ref': '5 MHz reference',
  'rad.c.d': 'Distance to the base station',
  'rad.c.n': 'Attenuation exponent n',
  'rad.c.bw': 'Bandwidth',
  'rad.o.m': '{v} m',
  'rad.o.km': '{v} km',
  'rad.o.n': '{v}',
  'rad.o.mhz': '{v} MHz',
  'rad.r.pr': 'Received signal (dBm)',
  'rad.r.noise': 'Noise in this bandwidth (dBm)',
  'rad.r.snr': 'Signal-to-noise ratio (dB)',
  'rad.r.cap': 'Shannon limit (Mbit/s)',
  'rad.cv.d': 'Distance →',
  'rad.cv.cap': 'Mbit/s',
  'rad.cv.low': 'Signal weaker than noise',
  'rad.say': 'The bandwidth is {bwx} times the 5 MHz reference, and the rate limit is <em>{capx} times</em> the reference. {tail}',
  'rad.say.near': 'Close in, with a strong signal, widening the bandwidth speeds things up almost in proportion.',
  'rad.say.far': 'Far away, widening the bandwidth also lets in proportionally more noise, so the rate barely rises: what is missing here is power, not bandwidth.',
  'rad.say.mid': 'Somewhere in between.',
  'rad.after1': 'The default is 1 km, n = 3.5, 20 MHz: the received power is about −55.5 dBm, the signal-to-noise ratio about 38.5 dB, and the Shannon limit about 256 Mbit/s. Doubling the distance to 2 km cuts the signal by about 10.5 dB, and the limit drops only to about 186 Mbit/s: the signal-to-noise ratio is logarithmic, so when the signal is strong the effect of distance is "softened." At 16 km, the signal-to-noise ratio is about −3.6 dB and the limit about 10.4 Mbit/s; now raising the bandwidth from 20 to 100 MHz only reaches about 11.9 Mbit/s. The same operation at 1 km takes 256 to about 1047 Mbit/s, a fourfold increase.',
  'rad.after2': 'That is why carriers build many small base stations in cities, rather than just offering wider frequency bands: <strong>what is missing at the edge of a cell is signal-to-noise ratio</strong>. For how a carrier wave carries bits outward, see <a href="../../../signal/sampling/modulation/#step1">the AM step on the Modulation page</a> and <a href="../../../signal/sampling/modulation/#step4">how FM resists noise</a>. The next chapter picks up the edge case here: when the signal-to-noise ratio is low, bits flip.',
  'rad.limit': 'The Shannon formula gives only an upper bound. Real 4G/5G systems fall several dB short of it, and modulation schemes have a highest tier, so nearby rates cap out. The transmit power, antenna gain, frequency and receiver noise figure in the model are order-of-magnitude values I chose. It also leaves out what often matters more in cities: interference from neighboring base stations and rapid fluctuations of the signal.',

  /* ───── 08 error correction ───── */
  'ecc.title': 'Three extra bits fix one error',
  'ecc.law': 'By sending a few carefully arranged check bits, the receiver can find and fix flipped bits without asking the sender to retransmit.',
  'ecc.p1': 'At the cell edge in the last chapter, noise occasionally makes a 0 sound like a 1. Asking for a resend means waiting a round trip, which is intolerable on a phone call. In 1950, Hamming came up with a method: add 3 check bits to 4 data bits, where each check bit covers a group of overlapping bits.',
  'ecc.p2': 'The clearest way to see it is as three circles. Each circle must contain an even number of 1s; the check bits exist to make each count even. If one bit flips in transit, each of the circles that contain it will show an odd count. Which circles fail is a pattern that points uniquely to that one bit.',
  'ecc.fig': 'Hamming (7,4) code: three circles',
  'ecc.how': 'The top row is the 4 data bits to send; click to change them. The second row is the 7 bits actually transmitted on the channel; click to flip one in transit. Watch which circles turn red, and how the receiver uses that to find and fix the bit. Then try flipping two bits at once.',
  'ecc.cvAria': 'Three-circle diagram for the Hamming code, marking each bit and whether each check passes',
  'ecc.lg.data': 'Data bit',
  'ecc.lg.parity': 'Check bit',
  'ecc.lg.bad': 'This circle contains an odd number of 1s: check failed',
  'ecc.c.data': '4 data bits to send (click to change)',
  'ecc.c.wire': '7 bits on the channel (click to flip in transit)',
  'ecc.c.clear': 'Undo all flips',
  'ecc.aria.data': 'Data bit {i}, currently {v}',
  'ecc.aria.wire': 'Position {pos} ({role}), currently {v}{state}',
  'ecc.aria.flipped': ', flipped in transit',
  'ecc.r.syn': 'Position indicated by the failing circles',
  'ecc.r.out': 'Data received by the receiver',
  'ecc.r.ok': 'Same as what was sent?',
  'ecc.r.none': 'None',
  'ecc.r.yes': 'Same',
  'ecc.r.no': 'Wrong',
  'ecc.cv.c1': 'Check 1',
  'ecc.cv.c2': 'Check 2',
  'ecc.cv.c3': 'Check 3',
  'ecc.say.clean': 'Every circle contains an even number of 1s, so the receiver concludes there is no error.',
  'ecc.say.fixed': 'The pattern of failing circles points to position <em>{pos}</em>. The receiver flips it back, and the data is completely correct.',
  'ecc.say.wrong': '{n} bits were flipped. The pattern of failing circles points to position <em>{pos}</em>, and the receiver corrects it, but the result is wrong: the Hamming code only guarantees correction of one error.',
  'ecc.say.wrong0': '{n} bits were flipped, yet every circle still happens to have an even count, so the receiver thinks all is well and accepts the wrong data.',
  'ecc.fig2': 'Sending continuously over a noisy channel',
  'ecc.how2': 'Each bit flips with probability p. The red line is the probability that an entire block fails when sending 4 bits directly; the gold line is the same for sending 7 bits with the Hamming code. The simulation keeps sending random data blocks at 300 per second, with the tally shown below. Drag p.',
  'ecc.cv2Aria': 'Probability that a whole block fails, with and without coding, as a function of bit-flip probability',
  'ecc.lg.raw': 'Uncoded, 4 bits',
  'ecc.lg.coded': 'Hamming code, 7 bits',
  'ecc.c.p': 'Probability p of each bit flipping',
  'ecc.c.reset': 'Restart count',
  'ecc.o.p': '{v}',
  'ecc.r.raw': 'Uncoded: probability a block fails',
  'ecc.r.coded': 'Hamming: probability a block fails',
  'ecc.r.sim': 'Blocks sent: failed blocks (uncoded / coded)',
  'ecc.sim': '{n}: {a} / {b}',
  'ecc.cv.p': 'Bit-flip probability p →',
  'ecc.after1': 'When two bits flip together, the decoder flips a third bit that was actually fine, so 3 of the 7 bits end up wrong: a code can correct only a limited number of errors, and this one can correct just one. In the chart above, at p = 1%, an uncoded block fails 3.94% of the time, while with the Hamming code only 0.20%: about twenty times fewer, at the cost of sending 75% more bits. At p = 10%, 34.4% falls only to 15.0%, because two errors in the same block become common. Real phones use much longer and far smarter codes (5G data channels use LDPC codes), which can get close to the Shannon limit from the previous chapter.',
  'ecc.limit': 'The model assumes each bit flips independently. Real channel errors often come in bursts, so the bits must also be shuffled (interleaved) before coding.',

  /* ───── 09 handover ───── */
  'ho.title': 'How a phone call follows you',
  'ho.law': 'A phone does not switch the moment it sees a stronger base station; it waits until the new signal is stronger by a margin. That margin exists to stop noise from making it jump back and forth.',
  'ho.p1': 'When someone calls you, how does the network know where you are? While the phone is on, it periodically reports which area it is in. When a call comes in, the network pages you on all the base stations in that area, and your phone responds through the base station with the strongest signal. If you are talking while driving, the call has to be passed from one base station to the next along the way; this is called <strong>handover</strong>.',
  'ho.p2': 'The difficulty comes in the middle between two base stations. As a car drives past a building, the signal can drop by a few decibels and come back after the building; this is called shadow fading, and each base station fluctuates on its own. Right in the middle, the two signals are almost equally strong, so "which one is stronger" is determined entirely by the fluctuations. If the phone always switches to whichever is stronger, it will bounce back and forth between the two base stations.',
  'ho.fig': 'A two-kilometer road with two base stations',
  'ho.how': 'The blue and purple lines are the signals from the two base stations received along the road, each following the attenuation model from chapter seven plus random fluctuations. The colored band at the bottom shows which base station the phone was connected to at each point; each gold vertical line is a handover. The default hysteresis is 0: switch whenever the other is even slightly stronger. Drive the car through at 5× speed and count the handovers; then raise the hysteresis to 3, 5 and 15 dB, and press "replay."',
  'ho.cvAria': 'Signal strength of two base stations along the road, the serving base station, and handover points',
  'ho.lg.a': 'Signal from base station A',
  'ho.lg.b': 'Signal from base station B',
  'ho.lg.ho': 'Handover',
  'ho.c.h': 'Hysteresis margin',
  'ho.c.replay': 'Replay',
  'ho.c.road': 'Try another road',
  'ho.o.db': '{v} dB',
  'ho.r.pos': 'Car position (m)',
  'ho.r.n': 'Handovers so far',
  'ho.r.worse': 'Distance traveled (m) while connected to a base station at least 3 dB weaker',
  'ho.cv.a': 'Base station A',
  'ho.cv.b': 'Base station B',
  'ho.cv.x': 'Position along road (m) →',
  'ho.cv.dbm': '{v} dBm',
  'ho.say.run': 'The car is at {pos} m and has handed over <em>{n}</em> times so far.',
  'ho.say.done': 'Over the whole trip there were <em>{n}</em> handovers, and <em>{worse} m</em> was spent connected to a noticeably weaker base station. {tail}',
  'ho.say.ping': 'Every unnecessary handover requires signaling traffic and can drop the call.',
  'ho.say.late': 'The margin is too large: the phone stays on an increasingly weak old base station.',
  'ho.say.ok': 'The margin just covers the fluctuations.',
  'ho.after1': 'On this default road, with hysteresis at 0, the whole trip has 35 handovers, nearly all of them bouncing back and forth in the middle. At 3 dB hysteresis it drops to 7; at 5 dB, only 1, at the cost of 28 m spent connected to a noticeably weaker base station. At 15 dB it is still 1, but that one handover comes too late: 198 m are spent with the weak station. A margin that is too small causes bouncing; one that is too large makes the phone sluggish. This is the same trade-off as <a href="../../../psychology/cognition/signal-detection/#step2">choosing a decision criterion in signal detection</a>. Real networks also require the new signal to stay stronger for a while before switching, and smooth the measurements first; these too are ways to deal with noise.',
  'ho.limit': 'Here there are only two base stations and one straight road. Shadow fading is generated with a common exponential correlation model (fluctuation 6 dB, correlation distance 50 m, both order-of-magnitude values); there is no fast fading, no measurement smoothing, and no handover for load balancing. "Try another road" generates a different set of random fluctuations, so the numbers quoted above hold only for the default road.',

  'part4.kicker': 'Part four',
  'part4.title': 'What keeps it all running',
  'part4.p': 'The last idea is not in the air but in the battery in your hand. It too is a measurement corrupted by noise: the phone cannot see how much charge remains in the battery, and can only read the voltage.',

  /* ───── 10 battery ───── */
  'bat.title': 'Why the battery level jumps',
  'bat.law': 'The voltage across a battery equals its open-circuit voltage minus the current times its internal resistance. When it is cold, the internal resistance rises, and the same battery runs out early under a heavy current.',
  'bat.p1': 'Outdoors in winter, the phone shows 30% and suddenly shuts off; put it back in your pocket to warm up, and it turns on again, with some charge back. While fast charging, the phone gets hot. These three things come from the same formula.',
  'bat.p2': 'You can think of a lithium battery as an ideal source in series with a small resistor. The ideal source\'s voltage is called the open-circuit voltage; it depends only on how much charge remains, falling slowly from about 4.2 V when full to around 3 V when nearly empty. The small resistor is called the internal resistance; in a phone battery pack it is on the order of a fraction of an ohm. When a current I flows, the voltage you measure across the battery is the open-circuit voltage minus I·R. The phone shuts down to protect the battery when the voltage drops below a certain value; in the model I use 3.3 V.',
  'bat.fig': 'The same battery, cold and warm',
  'bat.how': 'In the upper chart, the horizontal axis is the charge actually left in the battery; the blue line is the open-circuit voltage; the gold line is the voltage across the terminals under load; the red dashed line is the shutdown voltage; the red shaded area is charge that cannot be drawn out at this current and temperature. The lower chart records the true charge (gray) and the charge shown by the phone (gold). The default is −10 °C, 1 A, with 50% actually remaining. Drag the current and temperature, or press "start using the phone" to let it discharge.',
  'bat.cvAria': 'Open-circuit voltage, loaded voltage and shutdown voltage as functions of charge',
  'bat.cv2Aria': 'True charge and displayed charge over time',
  'bat.lg.ocv': 'Open-circuit voltage',
  'bat.lg.v': 'Voltage across the terminals under load',
  'bat.lg.cut': 'Shutdown voltage, and charge that cannot be drawn out',
  'bat.lg.true': 'True charge (lower chart)',
  'bat.c.i': 'Current drawn by the phone',
  'bat.c.t': 'Temperature',
  'bat.c.play': 'Start using the phone',
  'bat.c.pause': 'Pause',
  'bat.c.step': 'Run 10 minutes',
  'bat.c.reset': 'Back to 50%',
  'bat.o.a': '{v} A',
  'bat.o.c': '{v} °C',
  'bat.r.q': 'True charge remaining in the battery',
  'bat.r.shown': 'Charge shown by the phone',
  'bat.r.ocv': 'Open-circuit voltage (V)',
  'bat.r.drop': 'I·R voltage drop (V)',
  'bat.r.v': 'Voltage across terminals (V)',
  'bat.r.heat': 'Heat from internal resistance I²R (W)',
  'bat.r.off': 'Off',
  'bat.cv.q': 'True charge →',
  'bat.cv.v': 'Voltage (V)',
  'bat.cv.cut': 'Shutdown {v} V',
  'bat.cv.min': 'Minutes →',
  'bat.say.on': 'The battery actually has {q} left, its internal resistance at this temperature is {r} Ω, and {i} A of current pulls down {drop} V. The charge that can be used under this load, which the phone displays, is <em>{shown}</em>.',
  'bat.say.off': 'The terminal voltage is only {v} V, below the shutdown line. Yet the battery still holds <em>{q}</em> of charge: it is all still there, just unable to be delivered at this temperature and current. Warm it up, or use less power, and it will "come back."',
  'bat.after1': 'In the default state, 50% is actually left, but the phone shows only about 38%. Raise the current to 2 A: the drop is about 0.68 V, the terminal voltage about 3.16 V, below the shutdown line; the phone turns off while half the battery\'s charge remains. At the same 2 A, raise the temperature to 25 °C: the internal resistance is smaller, and the display shows about 46%. The charge "jumps," but what jumps is not the charge; it is the charge that can be drawn out right now. The internal resistance also generates heat, with power I²R: at 25 °C, 1 A gives only about 0.12 W, while 3 A gives about 1.08 W: three times the current, nine times the heat. This is one of the main reasons a phone gets hot during fast charging.',
  'bat.after2': 'For how internal resistance limits the maximum power a battery can deliver, see <a href="../../../physics/circuits/ohms-law/#step4">the load step on the Ohm\'s Law page</a>; for the chemical reactions inside a battery, see <a href="../../../chemistry/reactions/electrochemistry/#step1">discharge in electrochemistry</a>. This chapter explains the mechanism only; it is not battery-care advice.',
  'bat.limit': 'The open-circuit voltage curve is schematic; the variation of internal resistance with temperature is my assumption (about four times larger at −20 °C than at 25 °C) and should be read as order of magnitude only. A real battery takes several seconds to recover from part of its voltage drop under high current; a phone\'s fuel gauge also counts the charge flowing in and out, which is more involved than the voltage-only approach used here.',

  /* ───── 11 ending ───── */
  'end.title': 'A troubleshooting table',
  'end.law': 'When you see a device do something magical, ask three questions: What tiny quantity does it measure? What is the noise? What method separates the two?',
  'end.p1': 'Looking back over the ten chapters, there were exactly ten measurements and ten methods:',
  'end.th.what': 'Function',
  'end.th.small': 'The tiny quantity it measures',
  'end.th.trick': 'Method for separating the signal from the noise',
  'end.r1.a': 'Location', 'end.r1.b': 'Signal arrival time at nanosecond precision', 'end.r1.c': 'Treat the clock error as an unknown and use one more satellite',
  'end.r2.a': 'Orientation', 'end.r2.b': 'Nanometer-scale displacement of a silicon mass', 'end.r2.c': 'Trust it only for the direction it does well; leave position to GPS correction',
  'end.r3.a': 'Touch', 'end.r3.b': 'Capacitance change of less than one picofarad', 'end.r3.c': 'Interpolate across several electrodes together',
  'end.r4.a': 'Photography', 'end.r4.b': 'A few photons per pixel', 'end.r4.c': 'Stack frames or merge pixels; noise falls as √N',
  'end.r5.a': 'Display', 'end.r5.b': 'Three numbers from the three cone types', 'end.r5.c': 'Reproduce only those three numbers',
  'end.r6.a': 'Compression', 'end.r6.b': 'The "surprise" in each pixel', 'end.r6.c': 'Store only the part that could not be guessed',
  'end.r7.a': 'Connectivity', 'end.r7.b': 'Power not much stronger than thermal noise', 'end.r7.c': 'Choose transmission speed by signal-to-noise ratio',
  'end.r8.a': 'Error correction', 'end.r8.b': 'Occasionally flipped bits', 'end.r8.c': 'Overlapping check bits point to where the error is',
  'end.r9.a': 'Handover', 'end.r9.b': 'Two fluctuating signal strengths', 'end.r9.c': 'Hysteresis margin',
  'end.r10.a': 'Battery level', 'end.r10.b': 'Voltage pulled down by internal resistance', 'end.r10.c': 'Subtract I·R before estimating',
  'end.p2': 'With this table, several of the phone\'s "quirks" can be explained on their own:',
  'end.fix1': '<strong>Between tall buildings, the position fix jumps around.</strong> The satellites you can see are crowded into a narrow strip of sky; chapter one\'s geometry magnifies the error, and signals reflected off building faces lengthen the distances. In an open area it usually recovers on its own.',
  'end.fix2': '<strong>Night photos look blotchy, and night mode asks you to hold steady for a few seconds.</strong> There are too few photons (chapter four), so it stacks frames in rapid succession; any hand movement misaligns the frames.',
  'end.fix3': '<strong>Photos taken in the dark have bigger files.</strong> Noise is unpredictable, so the compressor has no choice but to store it faithfully (chapter six).',
  'end.fix4': '<strong>When the signal is down to one bar, video stutters, and a faster plan does not help.</strong> What is missing at the edge is signal-to-noise ratio, not bandwidth (chapter seven).',
  'end.fix5': '<strong>In winter the display shows charge left but the phone shuts off; warming it up brings it back.</strong> The internal resistance rises, so the same current drops more voltage (chapter ten).',
  'end.sum': 'Put together, it comes down to one sentence: there is no magic in a phone, only <em>very small measurements</em> and <em>very clever subtraction</em>. It knows its clock is inaccurate, so it solves for the clock error; it knows photons are random, so it counts many times; it knows signals fluctuate, so it leaves a margin. The next time you pick up any device, you can start by asking: what is it measuring? How does it deal with noise?',

  'footer': 'Ten Ideas Inside One Phone · <a href="../../../">visual.integ.life</a><br>Models in this essay: two-dimensional pseudorange positioning (Gauss–Newton solution for clock error), spring-mass accelerometer and double-integration drift, electrode centroid interpolation, Poisson photon counting and multi-frame stacking, Govardovskii pigment templates and three-primary matching, left-neighbor prediction with residual entropy, log-distance path loss and Shannon capacity, Hamming (7,4) code, hysteresis handover with shadow fading, and an open-circuit-voltage–internal-resistance battery model. All are simplifications for teaching; sources and figures I could not verify are listed in BRIEF.md in this directory. Written by Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5).'
};
